# This file documents WHICH variables exist and what each is for. It must never
# contain a real value.
#
# The owner's record of the actual values lives in SECRETS.local.md at the repo root
# — gitignored (by name and by the *.local.md pattern), so it cannot be committed.
# It also notes, per key, which Vercel environments it must be set in.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase (required for all environments)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Auth – Google OAuth
# Client secret goes into the Supabase dashboard, NOT here.
# Supabase > Authentication > Providers > Google
#
# The Client ID is public and is used client-side for the native Google
# Identity Services sign-in (no supabase.co redirect flash). Same OAuth Web
# client as the Supabase provider. Add it in Google Cloud Console with
# majorcycle.com as an Authorized JavaScript origin, and list it under the
# Supabase Google provider's "Authorized Client IDs".
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email – Custom SMTP password for Supabase Auth is the Resend API key below.
# (Supabase > Project Settings > Authentication > SMTP: host smtp.resend.com,
#  port 465, user "resend", password = RESEND_API_KEY, sender noreply@majorcycle.com)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stripe (required for payments — F3)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server secret. Use a RESTRICTED key (rk_…, least privilege), never a full sk_.
# Test key (rk_test_/sk_test_) in .env.local + Vercel Preview; live key in Vercel
# Production ONLY. Test and live are isolated Stripe environments — never mix.
#
# The LIVE key needs exactly these permissions, derived from the real call sites
# (Stripe's rule: GET → read, POST/DELETE → write, and write implies read):
#   Checkout Sessions   write   POST /v1/checkout/sessions
#   Customer Portal     write   POST /v1/billing_portal/sessions
#   Subscriptions       write   POST + DELETE /v1/subscriptions  (list covered by read)
#   Prices              read    GET  /v1/prices                  (resolve by lookup_key)
#   Charges and Refunds read    GET  /v1/charges/:id             (dispute attribution)
# No Products / Invoices / PaymentIntents permission is needed — the webhook reads invoice
# and dispute data from the EVENT PAYLOAD, and signature verification is local crypto.
#
# CUSTOMERS IS DELIBERATELY "None" — do not re-grant it. It was granted on 2026-07-26 out
# of caution and dropped on 2026-08-01 after being PROVEN unnecessary: shipped code makes
# ten Stripe calls and none is customers.*. The open question was whether Stripe needs it
# as an internal dependency of checkout/portal session creation — it does not. Verified in
# the sandbox with an identically-scoped restricted key: all nine app calls succeeded,
# INCLUDING checkout.sessions.create with customer_email (the call that makes Stripe create
# a Customer) and billingPortal.sessions.create, while customers.create and
# customers.retrieve were refused with StripePermissionError. That refusal is the control
# that makes the nine passes meaningful.
#
# THE SANDBOX KEY IS SCOPED THE SAME WAY (fixed 2026-08-01, live-check Session 4). It was
# a full-access sk_test_ until then — pure drift, because the sandbox key predated the
# hardening. That mattered for the same reason the /api/analyze-dev finding did: a dev
# stand-in that behaves differently from production is a test that lies. Local dev is where
# every real Stripe call in Sessions 1–3 was actually exercised, so a customers.* call
# added tomorrow would have worked there and failed only in front of a customer. Both keys
# now carry the same five permissions, so local dev cannot do anything production cannot.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Dev harness ONLY — a FULL-access sk_test_ for the sandbox. Never set in Vercel, never
# read by shipped code (the dev-harness-key section of scripts/check-entitlement-gates.mjs fails the build
# if app/, lib/, api/, components/ or proxy.ts so much as mentions it).
#
# Why the split exists: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY above is deliberately restricted to what the app
# does, which is the point — but the test harness legitimately needs what the app must not
# have (test clocks, disputes, fake customers), and `pnpm stripe:listen` needs to open a
# CLI session and read GET /v1/account, neither of which the five app permissions cover.
# One key per use case is Stripe's own guidance. Without this, tightening the app key
# breaks the harness, and the tempting "fix" is to loosen the app key again.
#
# Leave EMPTY if you only run the app: stripe:listen falls back to STRIPE_SECRET_KEY and
# says which key it used. Everything else works without it.
STRIPE_TEST_ADMIN_KEY=
# Webhook signing secret (whsec_…) for /api/stripe/webhook. Test value from the
# Stripe CLI (`stripe listen`) or a test-mode endpoint; live value from the live endpoint.
#
# The LIVE endpoint URL must be https://www.majorcycle.com/api/stripe/webhook — WITH the
# www. The apex 307-redirects to www, and Stripe counts any 3xx as a FAILED delivery, so
# the apex would silently fail every event with nothing visible in our logs.
#
# LIVE ENDPOINT CREATED 2026-08-01: we_1TzaT1K8OQZXQEminyKXmO3M (livemode true, enabled,
# 13 events, api_version 2026-06-24.dahlia). Its whsec_ is set in Vercel PRODUCTION only.
# Proven on the wire with an unsigned POST -> 400 "Missing signature", which is the only
# available proof: Stripe cannot send a test event to a live endpoint.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# NOTE: no price-ID env vars. Prices are resolved at runtime by lookup_key
# (majorcycle_monthly / majorcycle_annual) in web/lib/stripe.ts, so the identical
# code works in both test and live (their price ids differ; lookup_keys don't).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email – Resend (required for transactional email)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Transactional sender for app-sent account emails (deletion scheduled / account
# deleted). Optional — defaults to "MajorCycle <noreply@majorcycle.com>". (Supabase
# AUTH emails send via Supabase SMTP; the /contact form uses CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL.)
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=
# From-address for /contact form notifications. Optional — defaults to
# "MajorCycle <support@majorcycle.com>" (reply-to is set to the submitter).
CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=
# Where /contact form submissions are emailed. Optional — defaults to
# support@majorcycle.com (a Cloudflare Email Routing inbox → owner Gmail).
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# App
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cron (account purge — F2 Part B)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret guarding /api/cron/purge-accounts. Set the SAME value as a Vercel
# project env var named CRON_SECRET — Vercel Cron then sends it automatically as
# "Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>", and the route rejects anything else.
CRON_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Paywall (F3 Step 10)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret marking a request as coming from our own server.
#
# /api/cycle cannot be gated by a session: the Stock Detail page renders on the
# server and fetches it over the PUBLIC url with no cookies attached, so a session
# check would redirect our own render to /login and blank every cycle section. This
# secret is what separates "our server rendering a page" from anyone on the
# internet. The proxy also injects it into /api/analyze once it has verified
# entitlement, so the screener can't be reached except through that gate.
#
# REQUIRED in production. Both Python functions fail CLOSED without it (503 + a
# loud log) — they never fall back to "open", which would silently reopen the hole.
# Any long random string; rotate by changing it in Vercel and redeploying.
#
# SET IT IN **BOTH** PREVIEW AND PRODUCTION, TO THE **SAME** VALUE. This is not a
# tidiness rule, and getting it wrong fails silently rather than loudly:
# `baseUrl()` (web/lib/cycle.ts) prefers VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, so EVERY
# deployment — previews included — server-renders Stock Detail by fetching
# *production's* /api/cycle. `fetchCycleAnalysis` then ends `if (!res.ok) return
# null` as a deliberate graceful degrade. So a missing or MISMATCHED secret does not
# raise an error: every Stock Detail page still renders 200, with every cycle
# section quietly EMPTY, and nothing in the logs to point at.
#
# Consequence for merge day: proving /api/cycle **401s a stranger** is only half the
# test. Prove equally that it **200s our own render** — load a real Stock Detail page
# and confirm the cycle sections actually populate. Checking only the refusal would
# let the product go blank while every security assertion passed.
CYCLE_INTERNAL_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local dev only — NEVER set in Vercel/production
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Skip Supabase auth in local dev so pages can be opened/verified without a login
# (gated to NODE_ENV !== 'production' in proxy.ts). Also unlocks the local-only
# /dev-fixtures null/edge gallery. Remove after verifying. MUST NOT reach prod.
# DEV_BYPASS_AUTH=true

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub Actions secrets (not used locally — set in repo settings)
# SUPABASE_URL=        (same as NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL)
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=   (also gates the CI Stripe webhook contract tests)
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# OWNER_EMAIL=         (alert destination for cron failures)
#
# Stripe webhook contract tests (e2e/stripe-webhook.spec.ts) — the CI e2e job maps
# these into STRIPE_SECRET_KEY / STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET. The tests SKIP cleanly until
# all three (these two + SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above) are present — which is worth
# remembering when reading a green run: a skipped suite is also a green one. Check the
# reported test count, not just the tick.
#
# STRIPE_TEST_SECRET_KEY must be the RESTRICTED rk_test_ — the same five permissions as
# live and as web/.env.local, Customers = None. It held a full-access sk_test_ until
# 2026-08-02, which made CI *more* permissive than production: the same drift Session 4
# closed for local dev, left open here. Never put the STRIPE_TEST_ADMIN_KEY harness key
# in CI; nothing in the test suite needs test clocks or fake customers.
#
# This is ENFORCED, not just documented: e2e/stripe-key-scope.spec.ts fails the build if
# whatever key is supplied here is a full sk_, is invalid, or still grants Customers.
# STRIPE_TEST_SECRET_KEY=      (test-mode restricted rk_test_)
# STRIPE_TEST_WEBHOOK_SECRET=  (whsec_ from the test-mode webhook endpoint)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
